Strike Momentum Strategy — Draft Rule Sheet
Concept by Nilus · Drafted for review · 27 July 2026
1. Overview
The idea is to trade a single option strike intraday, entering only when that strike's premium shows sustained upward strength right from the market open. The trade is protected first by a wide initial stop, then by a tighter stop that trails candle by candle once the trade is in profit.
2. Entry Trigger
Watch the premium of the chosen strike from market open.
    1. Note the strike's premium at market open (the “opening premium”).
    2. Track the premium as trading continues. The setup stays valid as long as the premium never trades more than 5 points below the opening premium — i.e., it stays flat-to-rising, or dips but never by more than 5 points from the open.
    3. If the premium ever falls more than 5 points below the opening premium, the setup is invalidated for that strike — do not enter (or exit if already tracking it as a pending setup).
    4. If the setup remains valid and the premium is moving higher, enter a long (buy) trade on that strike.
3. Stop-Loss Rules
3.1 Initial stop loss
On entry, place a hard stop loss 10 points below the entry premium. This stays in place while the trade is flat or underwater.
3.2 Switch to trailing stop
Once the trade moves into profit, replace the 10-point initial stop with a candle-based trailing stop of 3 points:
    • As each new candle closes higher and price advances in your favor, move the stop loss to 3 points below that candle's low.
    • The trailing stop only ever moves up (tightens), never back down.
    • Exit the trade the moment price hits the current trailing stop.
In short: 10-point fixed stop while the trade hasn't proven itself → 3-point candle-trailing stop once it has.
4. Open Questions to Confirm with Nilus
The concept is clear on the entry idea and the two stop-loss stages, but a few operational details still need to be pinned down before this is tradeable as written:
    • Candle timeframe — what chart interval (1-minute, 3-minute, 5-minute?) is used for the trailing stop and for judging the 5-point pullback rule?
    • “Moves into profit” threshold — how many points of profit trigger the switch from the 10-point stop to the 3-point trail? (e.g., immediately, or after +5, +10 points?)
    • Strike selection — how is “the particular strike” chosen each day (ATM, a fixed offset from spot, highest open interest, etc.)? Calls only, or puts too?
    • Instrument and index — which underlying (e.g., Nifty, Bank Nifty, a stock) and lot size does this apply to?
    • Time window — how long after market open is the setup evaluated before it's considered “invalidated” or “confirmed”?
    • Targets and exits — is there a profit target, or is the trailing stop the only exit? Any hard time-based exit (e.g., square off by a fixed time)?
    • Position sizing and frequency — how much capital/lots per trade, and is this a one-trade-per-day setup or can it re-enter after a stop-out?
5. Disclaimer
This document is a plain-language write-up of the strategy idea as described, for discussion and refinement purposes only. It is not financial advice, and hasn't been backtested or validated here. Options trading carries substantial risk of loss; confirm the open questions above and consider testing on historical data or in a paper account before risking real capital.
